<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="what-this-document-is">What this document is</h2>
<ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number">
  <li><p>This memorandum of understanding (MOU) records the arrangements between the Government Digital Service (GDS), part of the United Hampshire Department for Science, Innovation and Technology, and the participating United Hampshire Government department or other authorised public authority (the <strong>service owner</strong>). It governs use of the shared GOV.UH Forms service by that organisation. Acceptance is made by an official competent to act on its behalf.</p></li>
  <li><p>This is a service governance memorandum rather than an instrument conferring statutory powers. It does not make GDS the decision-maker for departmental services, amend citizenship or immigration law, determine data-retention periods or remove the requirement to approve individual forms. The parties must comply with their independent legal, constitutional, information-management and security obligations.</p></li>
  <li><p>The memorandum and its annexes must be read together. An organisation's acceptance allows it to undertake production-group onboarding subject to service assurance; it does not certify that every draft form is suitable for public use.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="about-govuh-forms">About GOV.UH Forms</h2>
<ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number" start="4">
  <li><p><a href="https://www.forms.service.gov.uhrblx.com/">GOV.UH Forms</a> is a shared United Hampshire Government digital service maintained within the GDS digital product estate. It enables authorised officials to build and publish online forms through GOV.UH Forms Admin and enables the public to complete published forms through GOV.UH Forms Runner.</p></li>
  <li><p>GDS is responsible for the common platform, its technical operation and user-access integration. Each participating organisation remains responsible for the purpose, lawful authority, information requested, approval, accessibility, support, receiving arrangements, decisions and authoritative records for its own forms. This separation remains in force where a technical supplier operates infrastructure.</p></li>
  <li><p>The service uses GOV.UH Signon for staff authentication and organisation-specific access. A group administrator may edit or manage forms within the permitted group. An organisation administrator may administer that organisation's Forms groups. A GDS super-administrator may administer the shared platform. No one obtains the substantive statutory powers of the service owner merely from an account or group role.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="starting-and-ending">Starting, reviewing and ending this agreement</h2>
<ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number" start="7">
  <li><p>The service owner appoints an authorised signatory and organisation administrator. The signatory records acceptance using GOV.UH Forms. The application retains the accepting account, organisation, type of agreement and date. The accepted edition is this current GOV.UH memorandum, and its edition date must be captured or linked in the organisation's administrative record.</p></li>
  <li><p>GDS may grant or revoke production-group privileges in accordance with the published onboarding and assurance processes. Group activation and acceptance of this agreement are administrative permissions only. Each form must pass its own legal, operational, information, accessibility and end-to-end acceptance tests before publication.</p></li>
  <li><p>GDS and the service owner must review the arrangements when there is a material change in service processing, infrastructure, security, retention arrangements or the competent authority. A new agreement edition must be made available to departmental administrators before renewed acceptance is required.</p></li>
  <li><p>On withdrawal of an organisation's access or replacement of the service, the parties must protect submissions awaiting transfer, preserve authoritative records, revoke access and conduct documented, lawful transfer or disposal. Termination does not authorise deletion of departmental case records.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="people-and-roles">People involved and responsibilities</h2>
<ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number" start="11">
  <li><p><strong>GDS:</strong> provides platform governance, product development, technical service operations, GOV.UH Signon integration, controlled deployments, platform security, monitoring, continuity, technical incident response and accessibility of shared components. GDS coordinates the platform-side handling of vulnerabilities, faults and service changes.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Service owner:</strong> establishes each service's legal authority, target users, eligibility, contents, purpose, lawful handling of information, privacy notice, support, accessible alternatives, applicant authentication, safeguarding, individual forms approvals, casework and decisions. The service owner is accountable for authoritative records and retention rules.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Organisation administrator:</strong> ensures staff have properly authorised GOV.UH Signon accounts, manages organisational form-group permissions, reviews access, arranges service-owner approvals and removes access promptly when no longer justified. Delegation to an official must reflect actual appointments and competence; it is not a general national membership requirement.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Other service operators or providers:</strong> perform only the hosting, infrastructure, storage, identity, messaging or support functions actually assigned to them. Their technical privileges do not confer departmental public authority.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="service-costs">Service costs</h2>
<p>GOV.UH Forms is operated as a shared Government service. The parties use the current approved Government funding and service management arrangements. This memorandum does not impose a user fee on applicants, create a departmental charge or promise an unapproved charging arrangement. Any applicable statutory application fee requires its own competent legal authority and must be displayed by the individual service.</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="security">Security and operational responsibilities</h2>
<ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number" start="15">
  <li><p>The parties must apply the Government Security Classifications Policy, appropriate access control, least privilege, two-step verification, encryption in transit, restricted submission storage, accountable administrative change, safe backups and monitored recovery appropriate to the information processed. Organisation and group access must not be used to circumvent restrictions on applicant or departmental data.</p></li>
  <li><p>GDS must maintain and verify the approved technical chain from controlled source to immutable release, deployed runtime, processing systems and records. The service owner's submission journey must be tested against the production-grade receiving arrangements, rather than inferred from a form preview or a draft in Forms Admin.</p></li>
  <li><p>Where a service uses a private object-storage submission route, it must transfer information securely to the receiving authority, preserve receipt and provenance, detect processing failures, reconcile duplicate attempts and provide an authorised recovery procedure. Individual forms may use different approved delivery methods; a delivery capability is not automatically enabled for every form.</p></li>
  <li><p>Material incidents involving unauthorised access, personal-data exposure, delivery failure, corruption, loss or unavailability must be reported, investigated and recorded under the applicable Government arrangements. The organisation remains responsible for its affected service and applicants; GDS manages the shared technical platform incident.</p></li>
  <li><p>Before publishing a form, the service owner must verify its data model, evidence and authentication method, accessibility, privacy information, response and status confirmation, operational reception and casework, user support, error handling, audit and retention. Where automated notifications are not configured or tested, they must not be described as operational.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="data-protection">Data protection and information rights</h2>
<ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number" start="20">
  <li><p>The service owner determines the purposes and lawful grounds for collecting personal data through its forms and is responsible for delivering the substantive public service. GDS processes information through the shared platform under approved Government processing instructions, subject to the legal obligations that apply to its own technical and administrative activities.</p></li>
  <li><p>Only information necessary for the specified service may be collected. Privacy information must identify the responsible authority, purposes, recipients or categories of recipients, retention approach, rights and contact channels. The parties must implement the actual published UH information-governance requirements; this MOU does not import an unrelated UK public authority or substitute an unverified privacy notice.</p></li>
  <li><p>GDS and approved technical providers must not access or disclose completed submissions beyond authorised operational necessity. Access to applicant information must be recorded and periodically reviewed. Requests to correct, access, restrict or otherwise exercise information rights must be referred to the responsible organisation and handled under applicable law.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="information-handling">Protecting and managing personal information</h2>
<ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number" start="23">
  <li><p>Data must be transferred to the competent organisation using only the configured and accepted delivery channel for the individual form. Shared infrastructure must not become the permanent authoritative record by retaining an additional copy. Technical logs and temporary submissions are distinct from the official casework and register records.</p></li>
  <li><p>Retention, disposal, archival transfer and legal holds are governed by the service owner's approved schedules and the current authorised technical retention procedures. This memorandum specifies no arbitrary statutory retention period. The relevant officer must maintain evidence of authorised disposal and preserve case records that remain subject to review, proceedings or a preservation duty.</p></li>
  <li><p>The parties must ensure the ability to detect failed transfer, retry safely, reconcile receipt, provide a service status and support the resolution of complaints or incidents without exposing personal information in ordinary email or public logs. A technical dispatch alone does not establish effective service of a statutory decision.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="information-requests">Information requests and accountability</h2>
<p>The party holding the authoritative information responds to requests under applicable United Hampshire information-rights, records, privacy and accountability arrangements. Requests about the common platform are handled by GDS within its competence; applications, decisions and substantive departmental information are handled by the service owner. The technical platform does not determine whether information is legally disclosable.</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="annex-a">Annex A: Definitions</h2>
<p><strong>GDS:</strong> Government Digital Service, an internal organisation of the United Hampshire Department for Science, Innovation and Technology. <strong>GOV.UH Forms:</strong> the shared form-authoring and public form-completion service. <strong>Service owner:</strong> the Government department or other competent participating public authority responsible for the individual service. <strong>Authoritative case record:</strong> the record maintained by the public authority exercising the underlying function. <strong>Submission:</strong> information supplied by a person through an individually published form. <strong>Production group:</strong> a Forms Admin group authorised to work with publishable services, not an authorisation to publish any unfinished form.</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="annex-b">Annex B: Service components and providers</h2>
<p>The GOV.UH Forms service uses the maintained GOV.UH Forms Admin and Runner applications, GOV.UH Signon, its approved publishing and deployment machinery and the infrastructure and storage in the controlled deployment register. The currently verified GOV.UH Forms Admin and Runner deployment uses the authorised OVHcloud infrastructure. The Interior Office citizenship intake uses restricted Garage object storage and its departmental casework service. Other form delivery services may differ and require their own recorded acceptance. The exact database, storage, backup, hosting and support provider inventory is controlled by GDS and the receiving organisation; the presence of inherited UK software does not establish that the United Kingdom's GDS, its separate UK notification service or its hosting providers operate GOV.UH.</p>
<p>Any new subprocessor or technical provider handling personal information must be identified, evaluated and recorded before processing. The platform technical operator must maintain a current list of approved dependencies and notify service owners of a material change affecting security, location, processing or continuity.</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="annex-c">Annex C: Records and retention</h2>
<p>The service owner's official application, status, decision, correspondence and statutory-register records are held by that authority in its approved systems. Forms Admin drafts, Runner submissions in transit, technical receipts, temporary storage, error logs, deployment artefacts and support records are different record classes and must not be assigned the same unapproved retention period. Disposal follows an approved records schedule, preservation requirements and documented technical deletion or transfer. If there is no approved retention rule for a class, disposal must remain suspended pending competent determination.</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="annex-d">Annex D: Schedule of processing</h2>
<p><strong>Subject matter:</strong> collection, validation, presentation, restricted technical handling and transfer of information supplied to approved Government form services. <strong>Duration:</strong> for the operational and retention periods authorised for the specific service and technical component. <strong>Data subjects:</strong> people and representatives using approved forms. <strong>Personal information:</strong> identity, contact and other questions and evidence specified by the individual approved form. <strong>Purpose:</strong> receipt and processing of an application, request or response for a lawful institutional function. <strong>Receiving authority:</strong> the competent service owner identified in the form and privacy information. <strong>Processing instruction:</strong> the service's approved form, delivery configuration, receipt procedure, privacy notice and processing record.</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="annex-e">Annex E: Technical and publication assurance</h2>
<p>A trial group enables preparation and testing only. A production group enables the organisation to submit individual forms through the service's controlled publication process. A form must not be made public until its own checks are complete. The minimum record includes competent authority, version, content and accessibility review, authenticated declaration where required, privacy and information handling, tested end-to-end submission and failure recovery, casework receipt, records owner, decision and notification operation, support and rollback arrangements. Publishing the form does not grant any nationality, immigration, residence or employment status or confer authority on the technical service.</p>
